{Indian Defense: Budapest Defense, Rubinstein Variation. A slight inaccuracy in move order — the bishop is better placed before this pawn commits.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e5 3. dxe5 Ng4 4. Bf4 g5 5. Bg3 Bg7 {Book so far. The plan is the usual one: finish development, then decide which side to open.} 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nc6 8. Nc3 ! Ngxe5 {Here the exchange is tempting and wrong: trading the good bishop leaves the dark squares bare.} (8... Nxf2 Rb1 Nxd1 {was the alternative — playable, but it hands over the initiative.}) 9. Nxe5 Nxe5 10. e3 d6 11. c5 dxc5 ?! 12. Qxd8+ Kxd8 13. O-O-O+ Bd7 14. hxg5 hxg5 15. Rxh8+ Bxh8 16. Ne4 Ke7 17. Nxg5 Bc6 18. f3 Bf6 19. Nh7 Bg7 20. Be2 Rh8 21. Ng5 f6 22. Nh3 Nxf3 23. Nf4 Ne5 24. Nh5 Bh6 25. Bf4 Bxg2 26. Rg1 Bf3 27. Bxh6 Rxh6 28. Rg7+ Kf8 29. Bxf3 {The rest is technique, which is another way of saying it still has to be played accurately.} Nxf3 0-1
